KG42.register("rules-f8a88713ea9bec3e6d76c018",{"id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R004","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Energy-trade execution","process_stage":"activation","market_meaning":"The CSP can purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets hosting its IDCs. Scope: The proposed CSP framework and the local energy markets hosting its geographically distributed IDCs. Condition: Through scheduling computing requests and controlling on-site energy-resource outputs; purchases concern deficit energy and sales concern surplus energy. Local energy trading is disabled in Case 2; the CSP can purchase energy only from the grid. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. Condition: Case 2 omits the local energy trading mechanism. The CSP can still purchase energy from local energy markets but cannot sell energy to them in Case 3. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. Condition: BESSs and renewable energy sources are absent in Case 3.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function execution.energy_trade under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["execution.energy_trade"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Benchmark Case 2 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. | Benchmark Case 3 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. | The proposed CSP framework and the local energy markets hosting its geographically distributed IDCs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-E19"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP can purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers through the local energy markets hosting its IDCs. Scope: The proposed CSP framework and the local energy markets hosting its geographically distributed IDCs. Condition: Through scheduling computing requests and controlling on-site energy-resource outputs; purchases concern deficit energy and sales concern surplus energy.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local energy trading is disabled in Case 2; the CSP can purchase energy only from the grid. Scope: Benchmark Case 2 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. Condition: Case 2 omits the local energy trading mechanism.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The CSP can still purchase energy from local energy markets but cannot sell energy to them in Case 3. Scope: Benchmark Case 3 in the comparison of CSP energy-management cases. Condition: BESSs and renewable energy sources are absent in Case 3.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 System overview","anchor_text":"a ir In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The gy IDCs are geographically ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, we consider a CSP that manages K networked IDCs. The IDCs are geographically distributed in K different areas, where there is a local energy market in each area. The K networked IDCs can receive end users’ computing requests delivered by W third-party communication infrastructure providers. Each IDC is considered to be equipped with the following on-site IT and energy resources: (i) a number of servers that process computing requests; (ii) RESs, including photovoltaic solar panels and/or wind turbines; and (iii) a BESS that is used to accommodate the on-site renewable energy and provide energy backup support to the IDC. Through properly scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources, the CSP can alter the temporal-spatial power consumption and production distribution of the IDC network. This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs. Such vision is depicted in Fig. 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.2 Energy management results","anchor_text":"each IDC s connected power line is set at 500 kW. All programs are implemented by Matlab and execute","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For validation purpose, we compare the proposed energy management model with 3 benchmark cases:\n• Case 1 – the proposed model;\n• Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid, while the surplus energy generated by the IDCs will be fed back into the grid without subsidy;\n• Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets, but it cannot sell energy to the markets due to the absence of these facilities.\n• Case 4 – without temporal scheduling of computing requests. That is, each request is allocated to an IDC and is immediately processed in its arrival time slot."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"The rule's source-supported content is an activation/case-toggle statement about whether local-market buying and selling are available. The same-mechanism evidence v141-b-g01-E04 and v141-b-g02-E19 does not define an execution-level decision variable or domain specific to execution.energy_trade. Source variables such as P_k^N(t), pi_k^T(t), E_k^T(t), and C_k^DEM(t) are used by the pricing and settlement rules; importing them here would move facts from other frozen rules.","empty_theta_reason":"No numeric or symbolic parameter is defined by the source specifically for execution.energy_trade. Values such as retail price, reference price/demand, elasticity, line capacity, and BESS parameters belong to the pricing, clearing, and settlement rules. The benchmark case labels are scenario labels, not parameters of an execution-level formula."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"Through properly scheduling computing requests in the IDC network and controlling power outputs of the on-site energy resources, the CSP can alter the temporal-spatial power consumption and production distribution of the IDC network. This, in turn, allows the CSP to purchase deficit energy from and sell surplus energy to other end energy prosumers (e.g. buildings and electric vehicles) via multiple local energy markets that host the IDCs."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 2 – without local energy trading mechanism. That is, the CSP can only purchase energy from the grid, while the surplus energy generated by the IDCs will be fed back into the grid without subsidy;"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","quote":"Case 3 – without BESSs and renewable energy sources. In this case, the CSP can still purchase energy from both the grid and the local energy markets, but it cannot sell energy to the markets due to the absence of these facilities."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"34930a68cdc944bfa22e7cbf4454cfb38940c003766adc443a51f36bd40cc9e1","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule consists of three procedural statements: a qualitative capability to purchase deficit energy and sell surplus energy through the local energy markets hosting the CSP's IDCs, and two benchmark-case exclusions (Case 2 disables local energy trading; Case 3 permits local-market purchases but not sales). The supplied same-mechanism evidence v141-b-g01-E04 and v141-b-g02-E19 describe these capabilities and case toggles in prose. The source's quantitative energy-trading equations are scoped to other frozen rules: Eqs. (26)-(27) are price/net-load sign constraints under v141-c-R003, and Eq. (14) defines settlement.payment under v141-c-R009. No source equation or source-defined execution variable is scoped to execution.energy_trade itself. Thus this rule is non-mathematical by source scope, not because extraction failed. B evidence remains unreviewed; no independent source review is claimed.","bindings":[],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
